# GitHub Actions

When creating or editing workflows under `.github/`, **never** add steps that write to the git repository.

## Forbidden in workflows

Do **not** include any of the following in GitHub Actions jobs or steps:

- `git commit` (any variant, including `-m`, `--amend`, empty commits)
- `git add` or `git add .` (staging changes for commit)
- `git push` that publishes new commits from the workflow
- `git merge`, `git rebase`, or other history-rewriting commands intended to update the remote branch
- Auto-commit bots, `git-auto-commit-action`, or equivalent “commit and push” helpers
- Scripts that stage, commit, or push on behalf of the workflow

## Allowed

- `actions/checkout` (read-only clone of the repo)
- `git diff`, `git log`, `git status`, `git rev-parse`, and other **read-only** git commands
- Publishing build artifacts, releases, or comments **without** committing source changes back to the repo

## Agent behavior

When the user asks for CI under `.github/`, implement lint, test, build, and deploy steps only. If a task would require committing generated files or pushing fixes from CI, **do not** add commit/push steps — explain the limitation and suggest a local or manual alternative instead.
